# Line-ending rules.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows with `core.autocrlf = true`, which
# rewrites LF to CRLF in the working copy on checkout. That is harmless for
# Python, Markdown and JSON, so nothing here overrides it globally — a blanket
# `* text=auto` would renormalize the 98 committed ledger files and change
# content that other checks hash.
#
# It is NOT harmless for shell scripts. A hook checked out with CRLF endings has
# a shebang line reading `#!/bin/sh\r`, and the kernel looks for an interpreter
# literally named "sh\r", which does not exist:
#
#     .githooks/pre-commit: /bin/sh^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# The hook then fails to run at all. Depending on the git version that either
# blocks every commit or — worse — is reported as a skipped hook, leaving the
# control-byte check silently disabled, which is the exact class of failure the
# hook exists to prevent.
.githooks/** text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
